,"text":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A column is any set of vertices: empty columns and repeated columns are kept, and a graph’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"self-loop is the one bit at its vertex, the hyperedge {v}. (NetworkX’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidence_matrix"},{"type":"text","text":" gives a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"loop a zero column; Godsil–Royle’s entry 2 is not a bit. A zero column would forget the loop.)"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix"},{"type":"text","text":" is the directed form; "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UndirectedIncidenceList"},{"type":"text","text":" is the sparse"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"representation of the same hypergraphs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Storage."}]},{"type":"text","text":" Both orientations are stored, as "},{"code":"AdjacencyMatrix","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" stores its rows: n rows of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"⌈m\/64⌉ words and m columns of ⌈n\/64⌉ words, with the row and column popcounts cached. So"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"members(ofHyperedgeAt:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"code":"incidentHyperedges(of:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" are each one "},{"code":"BitSet","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" copy of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"⌈n\/64⌉ or ⌈m\/64⌉ words, a cell edit writes two bits in O(1), and "},{"code":"transposed()","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", the dual,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"swaps the two in O(1). The cost is 2·n·m\/8 bytes: "},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"the matrix is for dense or small"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"incidence"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (spectral work at n·m up to about 10⁹, hypergraphs with large hyperedges). Sparse"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"incidence belongs in "},{"code":"UndirectedIncidenceList","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Equality compares cell for cell, with equal shape: column order is observable, because a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"column’s position is what a spectral caller indexes by. (","type":"text"},{"code":"UndirectedIncidenceList","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"’s equality","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"ignores positions; convert to compare that way.)","type":"text"}]}]}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/incidencematrixmodule\/incidencematrix"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/documentation\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/IncidenceMatrix","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"kind":"symbol","references":{"doc://IncidenceMatrixModule/s16SendableMetatypeP":{"title":"Swift.SendableMetatype","identifier":"doc:\/\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/s16SendableMetatypeP","type":"unresolvable"},"doc://IncidenceMatrixModule/documentation/IncidenceMatrixModule/IncidenceMatrix/init(_:)-5i569":{"kind":"symbol","title":"init(_:)","abstract":[{"text":"The unoriented incidence matrix of a directed graph: each edge’s column has both ends","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"(NetworkX’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"oriented=False","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"), a self-loop’s one bit. Rows by vertex index, columns in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edges"},{"type":"text","text":" order."}],"fragments":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"init"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"kind":"keyword","text":"some"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"DirectedGraph","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols08DirectedA0P"},{"kind":"text","text":")"}],"identif